<item rdf:about="http://blog.sesse.net/blog/tech/2026-10-21-23-19_decompilation_patterns_part_18_signed_division.html">
	<title>Steinar H. Gunderson: Decompilation patterns, part 18: Signed division</title>
	<link>http://blog.sesse.net/blog/tech/2026-10-21-23-19_decompilation_patterns_part_18_signed_division.html</link>
     <content:encoded>&lt;p&gt;As promised, the last one (for now?), and this one blew my mind the first
time I saw it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;    var_v0 = sp4014;
    if ((s32) sp4014 &amp;lt; 0) {
        var_v0 = sp4014 + 0x7FF;
    }
    temp_s1 = 0x800 - (sp4014 - (((s32) var_v0 &amp;gt;&amp;gt; 0xB) &amp;lt;&amp;lt; 0xB));
&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There&#39;s actually two things going on here. First, the compiler
is faking a signed division using shifting (which rounds down) as a building block:
If the value is negative, then you add a bias to make shift into round-up.
(The right-shift by 0xB, or 11, is exactly a division by 0x800.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So this means what we&#39;re looking at is:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;    var_v0 = (s32) sp4014 / 0x800;
    temp_s1 = 0x800 - (sp4014 - var_v0 * 0x800);
&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;p&gt;which reduces further to:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;    temp_s1 = 0x800 - ((s32) sp4014 % 0x800);
&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tada. That&#39;s it. Now you know all the tricks, except those that I don&#39;t know
or that I forgot. You don&#39;t need the LLM. Have fun. :-)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Edit: Corrected a thinko; of course, MIPS has signed division.
This is for power-of-two optimizations only.&lt;/p&gt;</content:encoded> 
	<dc:date>2026-10-21T21:30:20+00:00</dc:date>
	<dc:creator>Steinar H. Gunderson</dc:creator>
</item> 
<item rdf:about="http://blog.sesse.net/blog/tech/2026-10-20-23-33_decompilation_patterns_part_17_loop_rotation.html">
	<title>Steinar H. Gunderson: Decompilation patterns, part 17: Loop rotation</title>
	<link>http://blog.sesse.net/blog/tech/2026-10-20-23-33_decompilation_patterns_part_17_loop_rotation.html</link>
     <content:encoded>&lt;p&gt;Occasionally, you&#39;ll see code like this (after initial cleanup):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;    var_v0_2 = crc &amp;amp; 0x80000000;
    do {
       if (var_v0_2 != 0) {
           crc = (crc &amp;lt;&amp;lt; 1) ^ 0x1EDC6F41;
       } else {
           crc &amp;lt;&amp;lt;= 1;
       }
       var_a0 += 1;
       var_v0_2 = crc &amp;amp; 0x80000000;
    } while (var_a0 &amp;lt; 8U);
&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The thing with &lt;code&gt;var_v0_2&lt;/code&gt; is unnatural. GCC sometimes does these loop rotation tricks, seemingly
to save registers (don&#39;t ask me how exactly it does). You want to un-rotate
back into:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;    do {
       var_v0_2 = crc &amp;amp; 0x80000000;
       if (var_v0_2 != 0) {
           crc = (crc &amp;lt;&amp;lt; 1) ^ 0x1EDC6F41;
       } else {
           crc &amp;lt;&amp;lt;= 1;
       }
       var_a0 += 1;
    } while (var_a0 &amp;lt; 8U);
&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;p&gt;and then of course, you can clean up the rest:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;for (i = 0; i &amp;lt; 8; ++i) {
    if (crc &amp;amp; 0x80000000) {
        crc = (crc &amp;lt;&amp;lt; 1) ^ 0x1EDC6F41;
    } else {
        crc &amp;lt;&amp;lt;= 1;
    }
}
&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Last one tomorrow, I think.&lt;/p&gt;</content:encoded> 
	<dc:date>2026-10-20T22:33:00+00:00</dc:date>
	<dc:creator>Steinar H. Gunderson</dc:creator>
</item> 
 

